'''Zong''' ([[Giokji]]: 總; [[POJ]]: ''Chóng''), officially the State of Zong ([[Giokji]]: 總國; [[POJ]]: ''Chóngkok''), is a [[w:Sovereign state|sovereign state]] in [[Eastern Fosia]]. It is situated along the eastern seaboard of the [[Fosia|Fosian]] continent, bordering the [[Shendan Ocean]]. With a population of over 393 million people, it is one of the most populous and economically significant nations in Adonia. Zong operates as a unitary parliamentary constitutional monarchy under King [[Kengjun]] and Prime Minister [[Tan Bopo]], with its capital and largest city located at [[Huwei]]. The nation possesses a deep and complex history spanning thousands of years, characterized by ancient philosophical traditions, maritime expansion, and a unique transition from imperial absolutism to a modern democratic state.

[[File:Zong_Capital_Cityscape.jpg|thumb|right|The modern skyline of Huwei, showcasing the economic miracle of the post-war era alongside ancient architectural elements.]]

==History==
{{main|Unification of Zong|Jade Sea War|Great Adonian War}}

The history of Zong spans over two millennia, evolving from a collection of river valley settlements into one of Adonia's most influential nations. From its foundational role as the progenitor of East Fosian civilization to its modern status as an industrial and maritime hegemon, Zong has profoundly shaped the geopolitical and cultural landscape of the Shendan Ocean basin.

===Etymology===
The name of the state derives from the ancient character for "gather" or "total," historically utilized by early tribes in the [[Jade Peninsula]] to describe the unification of diverse river valley settlements. During the classical era, the term became associated with the centralized authority of the imperial courts. In regional languages such as Sansi Cantonese, the name is pronounced as ''Zung'', while in the widespread Hokkien dialects it is pronounced as ''Chóng''.

===Prehistory and Early Civilizations===
Human habitation in the region dates to the [[w:Paleolithic|Paleolithic]] era, with archaeological evidence suggesting early hominid settlements along the fertile basins of the Jade River and the Pearl River. The earliest agrarian societies emerged around 5000 BCE, cultivating rice and millet. These settlements eventually coalesced into advanced regional cultures, most notably the [[Nakashima Culture]] in the south and the early animist tribes of the north.

The invention of the [[Donghai Script]] around the Jade Peninsula marked the transition from prehistory into recorded antiquity. This early writing system, one of the oldest in Eastern Fosia, paved the way for the region's first organized polities and would later evolve into the standardized Zong characters that spread throughout the Shendan basin.

===Kingdom of Donghai and the Haizei Raids===
{{main|Kingdom of Donghai|Haizei}}

The [[Kingdom of Donghai]] (675 BCE – 285 CE) emerged as the dominant power along the northern coast of what would become Zong. Centered on its capital at Haicheng, Donghai controlled a vast maritime trade network extending from the Jade Peninsula to the archipelagos of the northern Shendan Ocean. The kingdom experienced a golden age between 200 BCE and 100 CE, during which it developed advanced shipbuilding techniques and established tributary relationships with coastal communities throughout the region.

However, Donghai's prosperity attracted hostile attention. Beginning in the 1st century CE, the [[Haizei]]—a confederation of maritime raiders originating from the islands of [[Yanbaru]] and Hapo—launched devastating raids along Donghai's northern coastal defenses. These raids, characterized by lightning-fast amphibious assaults and the use of highly maneuverable outrigger warships, progressively weakened Donghai's maritime infrastructure and disrupted its trade networks.

The death knell for Donghai came in 285 CE, when a coordinated Zhongyuan invasion from the west coincided with a massive Haizei assault on the coast. [[Queen Zhao]] was killed in the defense of Haicheng, and the kingdom collapsed after nearly a millennium of existence. The Haizei raids would continue for another century, fundamentally altering the balance of power in the region and compelling coastal communities to develop new defensive strategies.

===The Eternal Wars of Zong===
{{main|Eternal Wars of Zong}}

The period from approximately 150 BCE to 350 CE was characterized by near-constant internal conflict known as the Eternal Wars of Zong. This era of fragmentation saw the rise and fall of numerous regional dynasties and warlord states, preventing the emergence of a unified continental power.

The wars were driven by competition for control of the fertile river valleys and trade routes. Various kingdoms and tribal confederations vied for dominance, with alliances shifting rapidly and borders constantly redrawn. For a generation after Donghai's fall, the peninsula was reunified under [[Great Zhongyuan]], but the death of King Yuanxiong in 300 CE triggered the [[War of the Four Sons]], which shattered the imperial order into the successor kingdoms of [[Haizhou]], [[Nanyang]], [[Jiaozhi]], and [[Zhongyuan]]. The chaos of this period had profound cultural consequences, including the dispersal of Zong intellectual traditions and administrative practices throughout the Shendan basin as refugees and scholars fled the fighting.

===The Mabatok Period and Dagit Influence===
{{main|Mabatok|Kingdom of Kabisayaan}}

From approximately 400 CE, the northeastern coastal archipelago of Mabatok fell under the dominion of the [[Kingdom of Kabisayaan]] (later known as [[Dagit]]). This foreign occupation, led by maritime sea-lords practicing the [[Anitism|Anitist]] faith, represented the only sustained period of non-Zhongyuan rule over territory that would become part of modern Zong.

Under Dagit administration, Mabatok developed as a hybrid cultural zone where the indigenous Zhongyuan majority lived under a small Dagiti aristocratic minority. The occupation introduced advanced naval technologies and maritime trade practices that would later prove essential to Zong's own naval development. The province was administered through the Kaharian system—a decentralized network of personal loyalties and naval supremacy rather than bureaucratic land-based governance.

The last Dagit stronghold at Pulohan fell in 1123 CE, and the fragmented successor lordships that emerged after the collapse of Dagit rule were folded into the realm during the unification campaigns of the 13th century, marking the complete integration of the archipelago into the expanding Zong state.

===Unification and the Jade Empire===
{{main|Unification of Zong|Jade Empire}}

The unification of Zong was achieved through two successive dynastic phases that collectively spanned nearly 350 years. The process began with the [[House of Jian]] (c. 900–992 CE), under which the inland kingdom of [[Zhongyuan]] became the principal military power of the [[Jade Peninsula]]. King Jianwen (r. c. 900–948) built the first permanent royal army and opened the coastward campaigns with the First Zong-Haizhou Campaign, taking the provinces of Linhai and Beiyuan. His successor King Jianwu (r. c. 948–992) completed the subordination of the inland rivals and established the practice of annual campaign seasons, making unification a permanent feature of state policy.

The [[House of Yuanlong]], formed in 992 through the marriage of King Longhai (r. c. 992–1066) to [[Princess Yulan of Nanyang]], united the Zhongyuan and Nanyang courts and carried the unification from the interior to the coast. Under Longhai and his successors King Longshan (r. c. 1066–1128) and King Tianrui (r. c. 1128–1210), the royal navy was strengthened, the harbour cities were tied closely to the court, and the frontier kingdoms—including the sacred Tianist seat of [[Beilong]]—were pressed into submission. The dynasty also adopted the [[Societal Assembly]] from Nanyang, a governing institution that provided a mechanism for integrating conquered territories and legitimizing Zong rule.

The final phase under [[Tianwu of Zong|King Tianwu]] (r. c. 1210–1250) completed the unification. The Beilong campaign brought the seat of the Tianist religion under imperial protection, while [[Marshal Lanqiu]]'s southern campaign in the Zong-Jiaozhi War broke the last remaining centres of resistance and reconquered the southern Haizhou districts that Jiaozhi had absorbed. The reconquest of [[Mabatok]] folded the last independent polities of the northeast into the realm. In 1248, Tianwu proclaimed the establishment of the [[Jade Empire]], uniting all Zhongyuan territories under a single imperial administration for the first time in history.

===Golden Age of Zong===
{{main|Golden Age of Zong|Great Zhongyuan}}

The century following unification (c. 1230–1300) is remembered as the Golden Age of Zong. With internal conflicts resolved and borders secure, the empire experienced unprecedented prosperity under Tianwu and his successor Tianhe (r. 1273–1302). Agricultural production expanded through the implementation of sophisticated irrigation systems, while trade flourished along both terrestrial and maritime routes.

The capital at Huwei grew into one of the largest cities in the world, attracting scholars, artisans, and merchants from across Adonia. The imperial court patronized the arts and sciences, leading to advances in astronomy, medicine, and engineering. The Great Zhongyuan—the network of roads, canals, and administrative centers that connected the empire—was completed during this period, facilitating the movement of goods, troops, and information throughout the realm.

The era also saw the codification of Zong legal traditions and the standardization of the Zong script, which had by then spread throughout the Shendan basin as the lingua franca of commerce and administration. The philosophical traditions that emerged during this period—emphasizing social harmony, bureaucratic meritocracy, and the divine mandate of the emperor—would influence governance throughout Eastern Fosia for centuries. The court pursued marriage diplomacy with the [[The Jyunhap|Jyunhap]] federation and the [[Three Louyan States]], culminating in a personal union with [[Bingdu]]—a bond that would draw Zong into its greatest war.

===The Jade Sea War and the Triple Kingdoms Alliance===
{{main|Jade Sea War|Triple Kingdoms Alliance}}

The 14th century brought the greatest existential threat to Zong since the Eternal Wars of Zong. Around 1300, the [[Chengzhi Dynasty]] of the [[Kingdom of Daras]]—a rising continental power to the west—invaded [[Bingdu]], the southwesternmost of the [[Three Louyan States]], which was bound to Zong by personal union. Zong was treaty-bound to defend its dependency, and the resulting [[Jade Sea War]] (c. 1300–1390) raged across the [[Jade Sea]] for ninety years.

Zong fought the war at the head of a coalition remembered in Zong historiography as the Triple Kingdoms Alliance with the [[Kingdom of Sansi]] and the [[Kingdom of Shanjian|Shanjian]]. The coalition represented the first major Fosian defensive pact: Sansi, as the leading state of the [[The Jyunhap|Jyunhap]], subsidised the war effort and opened the federation's ports to the Zong fleet, while the Three Louyan States contributed militia and supply routes to the defence of the southwestern frontier. The alliance dramatically redirected Zong resources toward maritime defense, constructing a massive naval infrastructure that would define its strategic doctrine for the next five centuries.

The turning point came in the 1340s, when Admiral [[Tan Beng-huat]] seized the islands of [[Bihong]] and [[Hape]], severing the Darasian fleet's access to the northeastern Jade Sea. The war concluded with the Heping Settlement of c. 1390, in which Daras withdrew all territorial claims to Bingdu and the eastern seaboard and the continental border was fixed along the interior mountain ranges. The military victory, however, came at a strategic cost: denied conquest, the Chengzhi dynasty and its successors imposed a permanent trade blockade that excluded Zong and the eastern states from the overland and maritime routes to [[Illypnia]] and the [[Mesogean Sea|Mesogean]] world. Strangled out of the continental economy, Zong turned east.

[[File:Triple_Kingdoms_Fleet.png|thumb|left|A historical reconstruction of the Triple Kingdoms Alliance naval fleet preparing for engagement during the Jade Sea War.]]

===The Shendan Pivot and New World Colonization===
{{main|History of Zong (1390-1538)|Tamsui|Sinchew}}

The Darasian blockade outlasted the war and struck at the foundation of Zong's prosperity. The post-war decades brought economic collapse, the [[Jade Sea Plague]] of the early 1400s—which killed an estimated tenth of the population—and a prolonged Great Famine in the agrarian interior. Within the [[Societal Assembly]], four decades of factional struggle ensued between the Continental Faction, which sought a negotiated restoration of the Darasian trade, and the Maritime Faction, which demanded a strategic pivot toward the [[Shendan Ocean]].

Emperor [[Tianyang of Zong|Tianyang]] (r. c. 1430–1478) broke the paralysis. His eastern reforms abandoned the diplomatic effort to lift the blockade, restructured taxation toward maritime commerce, and authorised a sustained programme of state-directed oceanic exploration. From the early 1430s, Zong fleets crossed the Shendan Ocean and charted the western coast of the [[New World]]—the continents of [[Lurandia]] and [[Abyala]]—establishing seasonal trading contacts with the indigenous peoples of the coast.

Colonisation followed. [[Xinhai]] (1456), the first permanent settlement, failed against organised indigenous resistance and was reduced to a fortified trading post. [[Anping]] (1474), on the northwestern coast of Lurandia, prospered and became the seat of the [[Louyang]] colony. [[Tamsui]] (1538), founded northwest of Anping as a penal settlement and plantation, grew into a turbulent frontier city where fortunes were made or lives were lost. When Tamsui's officials refused the authority of the colonial government at Anping in the [[Tamsui Plot]] of 1543, the court divided the territory along the [[Qingjiang]] river: the northwest became the [[Sinchew]] colony, the southeast remained Louyang, each with its own governor answering directly to Huwei. Louyang remained the wealthier and more established of the two, while Sinchew developed as a harsher, more militarised frontier society.

The colonies also became a critical nexus for the Fosian diaspora, integrating displaced merchants and artisans alongside the [[Sengoku Period|Sengoku Export]]—thousands of disenfranchised Sanese warriors, the rōnin of the archipelago's civil wars, who from the mid-16th century crossed the Shendan to serve as garrison troops, expeditionary forces, and security details in the colonies, particularly in Sinchew.

===Surplus Crisis and the Baiduist Usurpation===
{{main|History of Zong (1538-1606)}}

The flood of colonial goods into the Zong market produced a surplus crisis from the 1560s to the 1580s that devastated domestic producers and destabilised the agrarian economy. The crisis, compounded by the oppression of the growing [[Baiduism|Baiduist]] communities of the eastern coast, erupted into the [[Great Peasant Revolt (Zong)|Great Peasant Revolt]] of 1572–1585, during which firearms spread through the countryside and imperial authority over the interior effectively collapsed.

In c. 1585, the Baiduist usurper [[Foyuan of Zong|Foyuan]] of the [[House of Canglan]] seized the throne and scrapped the Tianist religious order. His four-year reign provoked immediate elite resistance and ended with the [[Yuanlong Restoration]] of 1587–1589, which overthrew and executed him. The restored emperor, [[Tianren of Zong|Tianren]] (r. 1589–c. 1605), pursued a policy of Baiduist integration, making concessions that formally expanded the Societal Assembly's legislative authority, but he was assassinated in the [[Canglan Rebellion]] of c. 1605. His brother [[Tianxiao of Zong|Tianxiao]] (r. c. 1605–1643), convinced that coexistence was impossible, issued the [[Baiduist Expulsion from Zong|Baiduist Expulsion]] of 1606, driving over a million Zong Baiduists overseas—most of them to Sinchew, which the expulsion transformed from a penal settlement into a major population centre.

===The Secret Trade===
{{main|The Secret Trade}}

The colonial era also introduced a dark chapter in Zong history. To fuel Sinchew's plantation economy, the merchant [[Ong Sam]] (1545–1620)—who had observed the plantation system at the Burawi colony of [[Lacashe]] in 1572—initiated the illicit [[The Secret Trade|Secret Trade]] from the 1570s, expanding it significantly during Foyuan's reign. The system utilized pirates and mercenaries from the [[Sanese archipelago]] to kidnap peoples from [[Dagit]] and the [[Balausia Peninsula]], transporting them to Sinchew as enslaved labor. Because the Zong Edict of 1500 had officially banned slavery on the mainland, the trade operated in a legal gray area overseas.

The trade was finally suppressed in 1680, when diplomatic pressure from the [[Sultanate of Maros]]—whose peripheral territories had been targeted by the raiders for decades—compelled the Zong court to deploy its navy against the slave raiders. Smaller-scale raids continued in secret until the 1750s, by which time the enslaved population of Sinchew had become largely self-reproducing. The demographic legacy of this trade remains a sensitive historiographical subject.

===The An-Ming Understanding and Colonial Partition===
{{main|An-Ming Understanding}}

As Illypnian powers expanded into the New World following the [[Great Disappointment]] of 1564—when the [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] expedition of [[Berenguer de Montcada]] discovered the Fosian colonies—Zong faced new competition for colonial territory. The [[Sultanate of Maros]], a fellow Fosian power, also maintained extensive holdings in the New World, creating potential for conflict over overlapping claims.

To prevent colonial warfare and manage relations with Western powers, Zong diplomats [[Lim Ming]] and [[Chen An]] negotiated the [[An-Ming Understanding]] with Marosan representatives between 1605 and 1606. Signed in the port city of Haihia, the Understanding's three conventions formally partitioned the New World: [[Lurandia]] was assigned to the Zong sphere, [[Abyala]] to the Marosan sphere, and the [[Liaojie isthmus]] was designated a neutral zone. The agreement directly conflicted with the [[Abyala Charter]] of 1537 between [[Burawa]] and [[Volisania]], establishing a competing legal framework that proved Eastern Fosian states could compete with Western colonial empires on equal footing.

[[File:Haihia_Treaty_Signing.jpg|thumb|right|An illustration of diplomats Lim Ming and Chen An finalizing the terms of the An-Ming Understanding in 1605.]]

===Expulsion, Revolution, and the Constitutional Monarchy===
{{main|History of Zong (1606-1711)|Baiduist Expulsion from Zong|Merchants' Revolution}}

The expulsion did not end the Baiduist question. The [[Baidu People's Rebellion]] (1606–1623) convulsed the kingdom, while the Baiduist diaspora transformed Sinchew into a self-conscious colonial society. Zong fought the [[Lau Kingdom War]] (c. 1612–1615) against the breakaway state of [[Shansi]], which had supported the Canglan Rebellion, and repelled the [[Kawaei invasion of Zong|Kawaei invasion]] (1620–1624), in which Sanese forces under the [[Kawaei Clan]] attacked through Shansi.

The decisive transformation came with the [[Merchants' Revolution]] of 1643—a bloodless transition engineered by a coalition of merchant houses, colonial representatives, and sympathetic aristocrats within the [[House of Yuanlong]]. Convening without imperial summons, the Societal Assembly bypassed Tianxiao's designated Tianist heir and recognised a commercial-minded cousin as [[Tianrong of Zong|Emperor Tianrong]] (r. 1643–1681). The new emperor, who owed his throne to the merchant coalition, accepted the creation of the [[General Assembly]] and the formal participation of non-royal representatives in the legislature, completing Zong's transition from an autocratic monarchy to a constitutional one.

The new order survived the [[Restoration Attempt]] of 1653 and fought the [[Sinchew War]] (1648–1656) against the Baiduist colony to a negotiated settlement that left the crown's sovereignty intact while granting the settlers a degree of self-governance. Zong also weathered the [[Second Lurandian Native Wars]] (1650s–1670s), in which indigenous peoples armed with firearms renewed their resistance to colonial expansion. Under Tianrong the Secret Trade was suppressed in 1680; under his son [[Tianzheng of Zong|Tianzheng]] (r. 1681–1711) the constitutional framework consolidated on the mainland, even as tensions in the colonial sphere mounted toward war.

===The First War of the Isthmus===
{{main|First War of the Isthmus}}

The early 18th century drew Zong into the [[First War of the Isthmus]] (1711–1716), the first major military conflict between Fosian and Illypnian colonial powers in the New World. Its causes lay in two grievances: Volisanian interference on the rebel side during the Sinchew War, and the progressive closure of [[Sant Cristòfor]]—the principal port of the [[Isthmus of Balboa]] and the key transit point of the trans-isthmian trade—to Zong merchant traffic, a direct threat to the commercial interests the General Assembly had been created to protect.

In 1711, Emperor Tianzheng authorised an expedition to seize Sant Cristòfor. Zong and its [[Sinchew]] and [[Louyang]] colonies faced [[United Kingdoms of Volisania|Volisania]], its viceroyalties of [[Viceroyalty of Balboa|Balboa]], [[Viceroyalty of New Volisania|New Volisania]] and [[Viceroyalty of Tapuya|Tapuya]], and its ally [[Burawa]], while [[Breisland]] and [[Merica]] backed Zong against their Illypnian rival. Zong forces captured Sant Cristòfor in October 1711, but the redeployment of the Volisanian navy around Abyala reversed the naval balance and isolated the Zong expeditionary force within the captured port. The war ended with the [[Treaty of Sant Cristòfor]] (12 January 1716): Zong withdrew from the port but received the docking and passage rights that had been the original cause of the conflict, Sant Cristòfor was designated a free port, and the breakaway territory of [[Free Sinchew]]—conquered during the war—was reunified with the Sinchew colony. The compromise was read across Adonia as a Volisanian diplomatic humiliation and confirmed Zong's standing as a global colonial power.

===Internal Reform===

The 18th century saw the gradual introduction of Western technological and administrative innovations, particularly in military organization and industrial development. The General Assembly's commercial representatives steered the colonial administration toward legitimacy and sustainability, but conservative factions resisted rapid modernization, creating tensions that would culminate in the constitutional crisis of the early 19th century.

===Constitutional Transition and the 19th Century===
{{main|Constitutional monarchy of Zong}}

The 19th century brought an end to Zong's relative isolation as Western Iron Envoys—Illypnian naval expeditions—challenged the Fosian order. These powers imposed unequal treaties that stripped away tariff autonomy and forced Zong to modernize its diplomatic and military apparatus.

Rather than suffering the complete collapse experienced by some Fosian states, progressive factions within the Zong establishment carried through a controlled modernization of the constitutional framework in 1827, reconstituting the General Assembly as a modern parliament. The settlement preserved the imperial institution within the constitutional monarchy first established by the Merchants' Revolution of 1643, while creating a framework for political participation and institutional reform that would govern Zong into the 20th century.

===Darasian Interventions===
{{main|Darasian Civil War|Darasian Socialist Revolution}}

The early 20th century saw Zong intervene decisively in the affairs of its historic rival, Daras. The [[Darasian Socialist Revolution]] of 1904–1905 threatened to overthrow the traditional monarchy and establish a radical socialist state on Zong's western border. Zong imposed naval blockades and provided covert support to Darasian royalist forces, contributing to the establishment of the Socialist Republic of Daras under terms favorable to Zong strategic interests.

The intervention continued during the [[Darasian Civil War]] (1905–1908), when Zong provided military and logistical support to Loyalist factions seeking to restore the monarchy. The long-term blockade of Darasian ports during this period demonstrated Zong's continued capacity to project maritime power and influence regional politics.

===The Great Adonian War===
{{main|Great Adonian War|Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty of Mutual Understanding}}

Zong's participation in the [[Great Adonian War]] (1947–1952) was framed as a defense of Fosian resource sovereignty against Illypnian colonial powers. The conflict was a devastating maritime struggle that exhausted the imperial system and depleted the nation's resources.

The war was fought in coordination with Zong's allies under the [[Mitoyagi-Zong Treaty of Mutual Understanding]] of 1895, which had established a framework for cooperative defense among Fosian powers. Zong naval forces conducted extensive operations throughout the Shendan Ocean, protecting shipping lanes and challenging Illypnian naval supremacy.

The conflict ended with the Armistice of Okami in 1952. While Zong retained its sovereignty and core territories, the war resulted in the loss of most colonial holdings and forced a profound internal political restructuring. The imperial institution was further marginalized, and the transition to a fully democratic parliamentary system accelerated.

===Modern Era===
In the decades following the Great Adonian War, Zong transitioned into an industrial titan, emphasizing maritime dominance and economic partnership over territorial expansion. The current constitution, adopted in 1913 and significantly revised in the post-war period, established the highly robust unitary parliamentary constitutional monarchy that governs today.

Modern Zong remains what it has always been: the "Brain of Fosia." From its ancient role as the progenitor of script and law to its modern status as a digital and industrial hegemon, Zong continues to exert profound influence on the civilizational landscape of the Shendan Ocean. The nation maintains deep economic and cultural ties with neighbors including [[Okaiken]], [[Yanbaru]], [[Nakamizu]], and [[Dagit]], while competing strategically with continental powers over resources and influence in the region.

==Geography==
===Physical Characteristics===
Zong occupies a vast and topographically diverse landmass in Eastern Fosia. The geography is broadly divided into the rugged, mountainous regions of the west and the fertile, densely populated river deltas of the east. The Jade Peninsula juts out into the Shendan Ocean, forming a natural maritime gateway that has dictated the nation's naval history. The eastern seaboard is characterized by deep-water ports and intricate archipelagos.

[[File:Zong_Karst_Mountains.jpg|thumb|right|The breathtaking karst mountain landscapes of the western interior, known for their mist-shrouded peaks and deep river valleys.]]

===Climate===
The country experiences a wide range of climatic conditions due to its vast latitudinal extent. The northern territories experience a [[w:Humid continental climate|humid continental climate]] with freezing winters and warm summers. The central river valleys feature a temperate climate, while the southern coastal regions, including the Cantonese-speaking hubs of San-wui and Tyun-mun, are defined by a [[w:Humid subtropical climate|humid subtropical climate]] subject to seasonal monsoons and occasional typhoons.

